## 4. Stage 3 — Data Modeling & Feature Store Design

> **Interviewer Question:**
> *"Explain your data modeling approach and how you implemented history tracking and feature store delivery."*

### STAR Framework

| Component | Detail |
|:---|:---|
| **Situation** | ML forecasting engines required point-in-time historical feature snapshots without data leakage, while BI reporting needed fast aggregation across retail store hierarchies |
| **Task** | Design a hybrid data modeling architecture — Kimball Dimensional modeling for BI/analytics combined with Point-in-Time Feature Store snapshots for ML |
| **Action** | Implemented Kimball star schemas in `BIW_DWH` using surrogate keys, standardized Class Words, and SCD Type 2 history tracking. For ML, engineered a Point-in-Time snapshot model `f_pfs_event_long_snapshot` |
| **Result** | Zero data leakage during ML model backtesting; 100% data model conformance across both operational reporting and predictive analytics |

### SCD Type 2 — Core SQL Pattern

```sql
-- Expire existing active row
MERGE INTO BIW_DWH.DIM_PRODUCT tgt
USING WRK.DIM_PRODUCT_INCOMING src
ON tgt.PRODUCT_KEY = src.PRODUCT_KEY
   AND tgt.CDW_RECD_CURR_FLG = 'Y'
WHEN MATCHED AND (
    tgt.PRODUCT_CATEGORY_DESC != src.PRODUCT_CATEGORY_DESC
    OR tgt.BRAND_DESC != src.BRAND_DESC
) THEN UPDATE SET
    tgt.CDW_RECD_CURR_FLG   = 'N',
    tgt.CDW_RECD_CLOSE_DT   = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (
    PRODUCT_KEY,
    PRODUCT_IDNT,
    PRODUCT_CATEGORY_DESC,
    BRAND_DESC,
    CDW_RECD_LOAD_DT,
    CDW_RECD_CLOSE_DT,
    CDW_RECD_CURR_FLG
) VALUES (
    src.PRODUCT_KEY,
    src.PRODUCT_IDNT,
    src.PRODUCT_CATEGORY_DESC,
    src.BRAND_DESC,
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(),
    '9999-12-31',
    'Y'
);
```

### Point-in-Time Feature Join Pattern

```sql
-- Point-in-Time feature lookup — eliminates data leakage for ML training
SELECT
    e.ITEM_KEY,
    e.LOC_KEY,
    e.EVENT_DATE,
    p.PRODUCT_CATEGORY_DESC,
    p.IS_MERCH_RANGED_FLG,
    pr.PROMO_DISCOUNT_PCT,
    pr.PROMO_TYPE_CDE
FROM ML_TRAINING.EVENT_SPINE       e
LEFT JOIN BIW_DWH.DIM_PRODUCT      p
    ON  e.ITEM_KEY = p.PRODUCT_KEY
    AND e.EVENT_DATE BETWEEN p.CDW_RECD_LOAD_DT AND p.CDW_RECD_CLOSE_DT
    AND p.CDW_RECD_CURR_FLG = 'Y'
LEFT JOIN BIW_DWH.DIM_PROMOTION    pr
    ON  e.ITEM_KEY = pr.ITEM_KEY
    AND e.EVENT_DATE BETWEEN pr.PROMO_START_DT AND pr.PROMO_END_DT;
```

### 🗣️ Spoken Voice Track

> *"For data modeling, I apply strict Kimball Dimensional Standards for our analytical core, paired with specialized Point-in-Time models for feature store delivery.*
>
> *In our core warehouse, fact tables represent atomic transaction events surrounded by conformed dimensions like Product and Location. All surrogate keys are deterministic hashes generated via `dbt_utils.generate_surrogate_key` based on natural business IDs.*
>
> *To track historical attribute changes, we implement SCD Type 2 logic. Every dimension includes standard audit flags. When an attribute changes, we expire the active row by setting `CDW_RECD_CURR_FLG = 'N'` and closing the timestamp, then insert a new row marked as current.*
>
> *For our downstream Product Feature Store, ML models require training datasets as of a specific date in history. To prevent data leakage — where future information accidentally corrupts historical feature values — I built Point-in-Time feature event tables using timestamp range joins against our SCD Type 2 dimension states."*

